{%- comment -%}
  The hero — port of components/home/hero-carousel.tsx.

  The design's 600px band with a cream card over the photograph, and as many
  slides as there are blocks.

  Slides travel sideways rather than crossfading: every slide is parked one band
  width away on the side it will arrive from, and only the offset changes. The
  band already clips, so nothing extra is needed to hide the parked ones.

  Rotation rules, in the order they matter (all in assets/theme.js):
    - It stops on hover and on keyboard focus, so a reader can finish the
      sentence they are on and reach the CTA without it moving.
    - A touch stops it FOR GOOD. There is no hover on a phone, so the pause above
      does not exist there; the first touch, or any use of the arrows or dots,
      ends rotation for the rest of the visit. A slide that slid back out from
      under a thumb is worse than one that stopped.
    - prefers-reduced-motion turns rotation off entirely rather than shortening
      it. An auto-advancing carousel is motion the reader did not ask for.
    - Inactive slides are `inert`, so their heading and CTA are out of the tab
      order and out of the accessibility tree instead of being invisible tab
      stops.

  DRAG. The band is draggable too: pointer down, move, release, and the slide
  nearest where the pointer let go becomes the active one (see assets/theme.js).
  That was added deliberately and it amends the rules above — sideways travel is
  no longer only the script's to command, the reader can push the strip. The
  argument is the same one that stops rotation at the first touch: on a phone
  there is no hover, and the arrows sit in a row under the card that a thumb has
  to reach for, while the band itself is most of the screen. A drag is the
  control already under the thumb. It ends rotation for good exactly as a tap on
  an arrow does, for the same reason — a slide that slid back out from under a
  thumb is worse than one that stopped.

  `touch-pan-y` on the band is what keeps that from costing a vertical scroll:
  sideways belongs to the carousel, up and down stays the page's, and the reader
  never has to find a gap between slides to scroll past the hero. It sits on the
  band rather than on a slide because touch-action is resolved up the hit-test
  chain — one declaration on the clipping band covers every slide, the copy card
  and the controls inside it.

  A drag finishes in the same render pass every other advance finishes in, so the
  parked slides keep their `inert` and their `aria-hidden` throughout. A slide
  dragged half into view is therefore visible but still out of the tab order and
  out of the accessibility tree, which is what keeps "one h1 per page" true
  mid-drag as well as at rest.

  One slide renders as a still band with no arrows and no dots — the controls are
  derived from the block count, not drawn in. A single slide has nothing to drag
  to, and theme.js leaves it alone.

  Each slide is parked at its own offset in the markup rather than all at zero, so
  the band is correct before script runs instead of relying on the active slide
  covering the others. With scripting off the controls are removed altogether —
  see the `scripting: none` block in theme-src/theme.css — because arrows that
  cannot advance anything are worse than a still band.

  NARROW SCREENS. The band is 600px at `md` and up, and 70svh below it (svh, not
  vh, so hiding the browser's URL bar does not resize the hero mid-scroll). The
  copy card fills the width there, which the design's controls do not allow for:
  arrows parked at the band's edges land on the heading and the CTA. So below
  `md` the arrows and dots gather into one row under the card, and their wrapper
  becomes `display: contents` at `md` — one set of markup, each control exactly
  where the design draws it on a wide screen.

  ARTWORK. Both shots ship as theme assets, and hero-1.webp leads with
  hero-2.webp following. `image` overrides the asset once the merchant uploads
  their own; with neither, the labelled placeholder fills the band at its exact
  size, so a missing shot never breaks it.

  ORDER. That is NOT the design's order, and the deviation was asked for. The
  design maps its slots (slotId 'sf-hero-' + i+1) and puts the studio's
  Indonesian order-notes card first; this band puts the polaroid photograph
  first and the card second. Two things fall out of it and both are
  improvements, which is why it was not argued with:

    - Below `md` the card is dropped, because it is `text_art` (see below). With
      the card leading, a phone opened on the band's bare surface; with the
      photograph leading it opens on a photograph.
    - The card is full-bleed Indonesian type. It reads as a notice, and it was
      the first thing a first-time visitor met.

  The filenames now match the positions — slide 1 is hero-1.webp, slide 2 is
  hero-2.webp — which they did not before this swap. That is a coincidence of
  the reorder, not a rename: each file was named for the slide it first sat on,
  the two were swapped once already, and swapping them back put every name back
  over its own slide. Nothing in the theme reads the number in a filename, so do
  not start now — `theme/templates/index.json` is where the order lives.

  hero-2.webp is still the design's own export at 1200x675. hero-1.webp is NOT
  — it was replaced with the studio's own higher-resolution render of the same
  polaroid composition, 2730x1536, because the design's export was soft on a
  wide screen: the band is 100vw, so a 1200px shot is upscaled on anything
  larger than a small laptop and doubly so at 2x. The render does not carry the
  "Wear Label" wordmark the design's export had across its top, which is fine —
  the header already says the name a few hundred pixels above it. The alt text
  was written about the composition, not the wordmark, so it still describes
  what is there.

  LCP. Leading with the photograph moves `fetchpriority="high"` onto it, so the
  first paint now pulls 249KB instead of the card's 172KB. That is the price of
  the reorder and it is paid knowingly. It cannot be trimmed from here: Shopify
  serves a theme asset at its own size, and `asset_url` takes no width — the
  responsive srcset only exists down the `image` branch above, so the way to get
  one is for the merchant to upload the shot as an image setting rather than
  ship it as an asset.

  The width/height below stay 1200x675 for both. They reserve the aspect ratio
  before the shot loads and nothing else — the img is `absolute inset-0
  object-cover`, so its painted size comes from the band. Both assets are 16:9,
  so one pair of numbers is right for both.

  `text_art` marks a slide whose artwork is an image OF text — here, the second
  slide's order-notes card. It cannot be cropped, so it is dropped below `md` and
  the band's own surface shows instead: the band is taller than it is wide there
  and object-cover keeps under half the card's width, which reads as a mistake.
  The words belong in the alt text.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign slide_count = section.blocks.size
  assign many = false
  if slide_count > 1
    assign many = true
  endif
-%}

<section
  aria-roledescription="carousel"
  aria-label="{{ 'carousel.label' | t }}"
  data-carousel
  data-interval="{{ section.settings.interval }}"
  class="relative h-[70svh] min-h-112 touch-pan-y overflow-hidden bg-surface-muted md:h-150"
>
  {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign active = false
      if forloop.first
        assign active = true
      endif

      assign slide_image = block.settings.image
      assign asset_name = block.settings.asset
    -%}

    <div
      data-slide
      {% unless active %}aria-hidden="true" inert{% endunless %}
      aria-roledescription="slide"
      aria-label="{{ forloop.index }} of {{ slide_count }}"
      style="transform:translateX({{ forloop.index0 | times: 100 }}%);z-index:{% if active %}2{% else %}1{% endif %}"
      class="absolute inset-0 motion-safe:transition-transform motion-safe:duration-(--duration-lead) motion-safe:ease-(--ease-entrance)"
      {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
    >
      <div class="absolute inset-0 {% if block.settings.text_art %}hidden md:block{% endif %}">
        {%- if slide_image -%}
          {% render 'media', image: slide_image, fill: true, sizes: '100vw', label: 'Hero photo', class: 'rounded-none' %}
        {%- elsif asset_name != blank -%}
          <div class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full overflow-hidden bg-tone">
            <img
              src="{{ asset_name | asset_url }}"
              alt="{{ block.settings.alt | escape }}"
              width="1200"
              height="675"
              {% if forloop.first %}fetchpriority="high"{% else %}loading="lazy"{% endif %}
              class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover"
            >
          </div>
        {%- else -%}
          {% render 'media', image: nil, fill: true, sizes: '100vw', label: 'Hero photo', class: 'rounded-none' %}
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        The bottom padding is the room the mobile control row sits in; at `md`
        the arrows move to the sides and the card recentres, and with one slide
        there is no row to leave room for.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="absolute inset-0 flex items-center md:pb-0 {% if many %}pb-16{% endif %}">
        <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-content px-gutter">
          <div class="flex max-w-130 flex-col gap-4 bg-canvas/92 p-6 md:gap-5 md:px-11 md:py-12">
            <span class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">
              {% render 'copy', value: block.settings.eyebrow, label: 'eyebrow', inline: true %}
            </span>

            {%- comment -%}
              Every slide carries an h1. Only the active slide is in the
              accessibility tree, so the page still has exactly one.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <h1 class="text-display leading-display tracking-display">
              {% render 'copy', value: block.settings.heading, label: 'heading' %}
            </h1>

            <p class="text-body leading-body text-ink-muted">
              {% render 'copy', value: block.settings.body, label: 'body', lines: 2 %}
            </p>

            {%- comment -%}
              TWO CTAs, and the second one is the point of the whole band.

              The brief's first screen has to say that Wear Label does two
              things — ready-to-wear and custom apparel production — before the
              reader scrolls. So the primary button shops and the secondary one
              goes to the B2B page, and the pair sits on every slide rather than
              only the first: any slide can be the one a reader lands on, and a
              slide that drops the custom route is a slide where the second
              business does not exist.

              `flex-wrap` rather than a two-column grid because these labels are
              set at 0.22em tracking and `px-11`: SHOP COLLECTION and CUSTOM
              APPAREL side by side need 430px, and the card is 100% of a 390px
              screen minus the gutter. They wrap to one per row there and sit in
              a row from about `sm` up, which is the width at which they fit.
              Both are `min-h-13` so a wrapped pair is still two 52px targets.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if block.settings.cta != blank or block.settings.cta_2 != blank -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Assigned rather than filtered inline: theme check's
                UnsupportedFilterArguments forbids a filter on a `render`
                argument, so `href | default: ...` has to resolve first.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- assign slide_href = block.settings.href | default: routes.all_products_collection_url -%}
              <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                {%- if block.settings.cta != blank -%}
                  {% render 'button',
                    label: block.settings.cta,
                    href: slide_href,
                    variant: 'primary',
                    class: 'max-sm:w-full' %}
                {%- endif -%}

                {%- if block.settings.cta_2 != blank -%}
                  {% render 'button',
                    label: block.settings.cta_2,
                    href: block.settings.href_2,
                    variant: 'secondary',
                    class: 'max-sm:w-full' %}
                {%- endif -%}
              </div>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  {%- endfor -%}

  {%- if many -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      One row under the card on a phone; at `md` the wrapper stops being a box at
      all (`contents`) and its three children take the positions the design draws
      — arrows against the band's edges, dots along its bottom.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="absolute inset-x-0 bottom-4 z-10 flex items-center justify-center gap-3 md:contents">
      {%- comment -%}
        The arrows are painted at 52px, over the 44px minimum, so the press is
        `.wl-press-soft` (0.98) rather than `.wl-tap` (0.95) — five percent of a
        control this size reads as a lurch. See app/base.css.

        Which is also why they are no longer centred with `md:top-1/2
        md:-translate-y-1/2`. The press state sets `transform: scale(...)`
        outright, and it wins over the utility, so a press at `md` would have
        dropped the arrow half its own height. `md:inset-y-0 md:my-auto` centres
        it with margins instead — a definite height between a top and a bottom of
        zero splits the leftover space — leaving `transform` free for the press.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <button
        type="button"
        data-carousel-prev
        class="wl-press-soft z-10 inline-flex size-13 shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center bg-canvas/90 text-ink transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:bg-canvas md:absolute md:inset-y-0 md:left-7 md:my-auto"
      >
        {% render 'icon', icon: 'chevron-left', class: 'size-5.5' %}
        <span class="sr-only">{{ 'carousel.previous_slide' | t }}</span>
      </button>

      <div class="z-10 flex items-center justify-center md:absolute md:inset-x-0 md:bottom-7">
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          <button
            type="button"
            data-carousel-dot="{{ forloop.index0 }}"
            {% if forloop.first %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}
            {%- comment -%}
              The bar is the artwork; .wl-tap is the 44px target around it, so the
              design keeps its 4px rule and a thumb still lands.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            class="wl-tap inline-flex h-11 cursor-pointer items-center px-3 md:px-1.25"
          >
            <span data-carousel-bar class="h-1 w-4.5 bg-ink/35 transition-[width,background-color] duration-(--duration-base) hover:bg-brand/60"></span>
            <span class="sr-only">{{ 'carousel.go_to_slide' | t }} {{ forloop.index }}</span>
          </button>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>

      <button
        type="button"
        data-carousel-next
        class="wl-press-soft z-10 inline-flex size-13 shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center bg-canvas/90 text-ink transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:bg-canvas md:absolute md:inset-y-0 md:right-7 md:my-auto"
      >
        {% render 'icon', icon: 'chevron-right', class: 'size-5.5' %}
        <span class="sr-only">{{ 'carousel.next_slide' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Hero carousel",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "interval",
      "label": "Seconds per slide",
      "min": 3,
      "max": 10,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 3,
      "info": "The design rotates at 6s; 3 is what this build asked for. Rotation is off entirely for readers who have asked for reduced motion."
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "slide",
      "name": "Slide",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading" },
        { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Body" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "cta", "label": "Button label" },
        { "type": "url", "id": "href", "label": "Button link" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "cta_2", "label": "Second button label", "info": "The custom-apparel route. Leave blank and only the first button renders." },
        { "type": "url", "id": "href_2", "label": "Second button link" },
        { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Photograph", "info": "Overrides the shipped artwork below." },
        { "type": "text", "id": "asset", "label": "Shipped asset filename", "info": "A file in the theme's assets/. Used when no photograph is uploaded." },
        { "type": "textarea", "id": "alt", "label": "Alt text" },
        { "type": "checkbox", "id": "text_art", "label": "Artwork is an image of text", "default": false, "info": "Hides the artwork below md, where cropping would slice the words mid-sentence." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Hero carousel" }]
}
{% endschema %}
